but first labor and opposition parties  are threatening to launch proceedings  for contempt of parliament unless the  legal advice given by the Attorney  General to the Prime Minister on her  brexit plan is published in full so  let's talk now to the man behind this  circus Dharma the shadow brexit  secretary thank you for being on the  show this morning so just explain what  is it you're trying to do here well as  you've just said in nine days time  Parliament's got to take probably the  most important decision is taken for a  generation and it's obviously important  we know the full legal implications of  what the Prime Minister wants us to sign  up to so we had a debate about two weeks  ago in Parliament where we said we need  the full legal advice from the Attorney  General the final advice at the end of  that debate Parliament ordered the  government to produce that advice now it  is exceptional to have that advice  produced to Parliament but that was the  order the government at that stage two  weeks ago had the opportunity to vote  against that order and they didn't they  abstain so there's that order the  government now says we're not going or  looks as if it's going to say we're not  going to comply with the order so as it  were having pushed Parliament away for  weeks and months if it's now moving into  the territory of ignoring Parliament all  together and breaching an order of  Parliament then we're getting into very  very deep water and you're talking about  contempt of Parliament proceedings I  mean what does that mean that what can  actually do can you force the government  to publish it yeah I mean the first  thing I should say is I don't want to go  down this path I said to the government  two weeks ago and I said to them last  week do the right thing you've been  ordered to produce this advise just  produce it if they don't produce it  tomorrow then we will start contempt  proceedings this will be a collision  course between the government and  Parliament that then has to be debated  in the house and an order can be made of  contempt now as I say I don't want to go  down this path with nine days to go  before this fate we shouldn't be dealing  with contempt of parliament but at the  moment for the government to say the one  hand we're not going to vote against the  order being made and then to turn round  if it does turn around tomorrow and say  but we're not going to comply with the  order is to get themselves into really  deep water the government has said  that it will publish a summary yeah I  mean why is that not good enough well  you need the full hearing because there  is an argument isn't there that it's  important for the government to be able  to have your honest and frank  conversations with their legal advisors  of course it is and that's why the  convention is that the Attorney  General's legal advice is not disclosed  except in exertion in exceptional  circumstances and these are exceptional  circumstances now when we had that  discussion in Parliament I was offered a  summary now having been a lawyer for  many years I know I've seen a lot of  summaries of legal advice and summaries  I'm not as good as the full legal advice  and they tend to leave out the bits that  the author of the summary doesn't want  people to know about so we said no to a  summary we said we want the full legal  advice and that was there what we voted  on and at the end of the debate the  speaker asked whether the government  wanted to oppose that order and they  chose not to so I think your Brandon  Lewis on blatant maybe ask him why they  didn't vote against the order if they  now say they shouldn't have to comply  with it you mentioned there that you  have been a lawyer obviously had been a  Norfolk a very long time and how many  times have you made your legal advice  public your private legal advice to  clients well on a number of occasions  lawyers make their advice public but  we're talking about a different issue  here we're talking about the Attorney  General's advice to the cabinet on the  legal implications of the agreement that  the prime minister wants us to sign up  to I accept that is exceptional to have  that disclosed it has happened in the  past but is it is exceptional that's why  we had a debate in Parliament to say is  this the sort of case where it's so  exceptional that it should be disclosed  and at the end of that debate the house  could have divided we could have the  government could have voted against it  but it didn't so I can't come along now  and say well we didn't vote against this  order but now we don't want to comply  with I mean is there also an argument  that we effectively know what that legal  advice is coming well well this is the  thing I mean now this morning on the  front page of The Sunday Times you see  that whilst the prime minister in the  Attorney General on one hand are saying  MPs can't see this advice leaked bits of  the advice are now getting into the  media  that can only have come from the cabinet  this advice is only being with the  company so you've got this situation  where somebody in and around the cabinet  who's seen this advice has got bits of  it to the Sunday Times so the journalist  and the edges at Sunday Times knows more  about this illegal advice than I do  this really is so unacceptable and now  we've got this key vote in pawn yeah and  coming up and more than 100 Conservative  MP is indicating that they're not happy  with the deal on the table so I want to  know what you're planning to do if  Theresa May loses that vote will you  call it motion of no confidence in her  well we've got nine days to go we'll  have to see which way the vote goes but  it looks like a considerable number of  Tory MPs are going to vote against it it  looks as though you DUP or not and the  opposition parties not so I think the  Prime Minister as we all know is going  to struggle between now and that vote  and people sort of praise her resilience  at the moment I don't think this is  resilience it's just plowing or what  will you do if she loses that vote the  legislation that we've already passed  says she must come back to the house and  make a statement about what she's going  to do next now technically she's got 21  days to do that but she'll probably come  back the next day so we need to see what  that is but it seems to me that if the  Prime Minister has lost a vote of that  sort of significance then there has to  be a question of confidence in the  government so will you seek to move that  I think it's inevitable that we will  seek to move that obviously it'll depend  on what actually happens in nine days  it'll depend on what the response is but  if she's lost a vote on this  significance after two years of  negotiation then it is right that there  should be a general election because but  for the fixed term Parliament act the  convention was always if a government  loses what's called a confidence vote  something of such significance then that  government has to go and you clearly  want to see a general election that's  been Labour's policy for a long time and  but I'm keen to press you on the second  referendum yes question as well because  there it does appear you know from an  observers point of view that Labour's  position has been shifting that you've  been warming to the idea of a second  referendum is that something that you  let me just set out how we thought this  through because we had a good discussion  about this at our Party conference in  September and we tried to look at the  decisions but we would have slightly  confused discussion wasn't it less  people it was a good discussion of  course there's different views and a lot  of views over the two years of course  there are but what we wanted to do at  conference what we took leaders say what  decisions we got to make in what order  first is what are you going to do about  the deal where we're at that stage now  if it's not good 